    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Panama seceded from Colombia in 1903 with American backing so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> could build a canal across the isthmus.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Canal Zone</a> for most of the century, and military rulers governed Panama from 1968 to 1989.</li><li>Under the Torrijos–Carter Treaties Panama took full control of the canal on 31 December 1999.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The 1903 treaty granting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Canal Zone</a> was signed for Panama by a Frenchman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-bunau-varilla">Philippe Bunau-Varilla</a>, before the Panamanian delegation had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

